# Git LFS (Large File Storage) Guide

Git LFS is a Git extension for managing large binary files (textures, models, audio, video) without bloating repository history.

---

# Why Git LFS?

Git is designed for text-based files. Large binaries cause problems:

- Repositories grow extremely large
- Cloning becomes slow
- History duplicates full binary files
- Collaboration becomes inefficient

Git LFS fixes this by storing:
- Small pointer files in Git
- Actual file data in LFS storage

---

# Installation

## macOS
brew install git-lfs


## Windows

Download installer:
https://git-lfs.com

## Linux (Debian/Ubuntu)
sudo apt install git-lfs


---

# Enable Git LFS

Run once per machine:
git lfs install


---

# Tracking Files

Define which file types Git LFS should manage:
git lfs track ".png"
git lfs track ".jpg"
git lfs track ".fbx"
git lfs track ".blend"
git lfs track ".wav"
git lfs track ".mp4"


This creates or updates `.gitattributes`.

---

# Initial Setup
git add .gitattributes
git add Assets/
git commit -m "Configure Git LFS and add assets"
git push origin main


---

# How Git LFS Works

When you commit a tracked file:

- Git stores a small pointer file
- LFS stores the actual binary data
- On clone, LFS downloads the real files

---

# Cloning a Repo with LFS

## Normal clone
git clone <repo-url>


## Skip downloading LFS files initially
GIT_LFS_SKIP_SMUDGE=1 git clone <repo-url>


Then:
git lfs pull

---

# Checking LFS Status

## Show tracked patterns
git lfs track


## List LFS files
git lfs ls-files

---

# Updating Tracking Rules
git lfs track "*.tga"
git add .gitattributes
git commit -m "Track TGA files with LFS"


---

# Useful Commands

## Pull LFS files
git lfs pull


## Fetch without checkout
git lfs fetch


## Verify LFS files
git lfs ls-files


---

# Fixing Mistakes

If files were committed before LFS:
git lfs migrate import --include=".fbx,.png"


⚠️ This rewrites Git history and affects collaborators.

---

# Best Practices

- Use LFS for binary assets (textures, models, audio)
- Do NOT commit build outputs
- Ignore generated files via `.gitignore`
- Keep repository structure clean and modular
- Avoid frequently-changing large binaries

---

# Recommended Project Structure
/Assets
/Textures
/Models
/Audio
/UI
/Source
/Docs
/Build


---

# When NOT to Use Git LFS

Avoid LFS for:

- Large frequently-changing datasets (multi-GB)
- Build artifacts
- Cache or temporary files

Instead use:
- Cloud storage (S3, Drive, etc.)
- CI/CD artifact storage
- Dedicated asset servers

---

# Summary

Git LFS allows Git to handle large binary assets efficiently by storing pointers in Git and real files in LFS storage, keeping repositories fast and manageable.